--TEST--
`$uri->compareTo` is the lexicographic order over the seven component readers in their declared order — for every ordered pair of a corpus its answer is the answer the *first* reader that disagrees would give, and every one of the seven decides some pair — spec § 12, `rule:classes/comparable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::compareTo
// The compositional question, which is the one no row of a table asks: not
// "what did this pair answer" but "is the answer a function of the parts at
// all". The neighbouring total-order case names two orderings by hand (a
// scheme deciding before a host, a host before a path); this one predicts
// every pair from the readers and counts the agreements, so a member that
// grew its own comparison — over the text, or over a component the readers
// cannot see — fails here while still printing plausibly on any single line.
//
// The corpus is deliberately free of percent-escapes and of dot segments, so
// the only part of RFC 3986 § 6.2.2 the prediction has to reproduce by hand is
// § 6.2.2.1's case fold on the scheme and the host. The other two rewrites
// have their own cases.

final class Part {
    // § 6.2.2.1, applied to the two components it reaches and to no other.
    public static function fold(?string $held): ?string {
        if ($held != null) {
            return Core\Str::lower($held);
        }
        return null;
    }

    // Two `?string` components ordered as the class orders them: absent before
    // present, and `Core\Str::compare` otherwise, which is the only ordering
    // two `string`s have (`rule:types/arithmetic` gives them no `<`).
    public static function texts(?string $a, ?string $b): int {
        if ($a != null) {
            if ($b != null) {
                return Core\Str::compare($a, $b);
            }
            return 1;
        }
        if ($b != null) {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    // The port is the one component that is a number, so it is the one the
    // prediction cannot make out of `Core\Str::compare`.
    public static function ports(?int $a, ?int $b): int {
        if ($a != null) {
            if ($b != null) {
                if ($a < $b) { return 0 - 1; }
                if ($a > $b) { return 1; }
                return 0;
            }
            return 1;
        }
        if ($b != null) {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    // Component `$index` of the two references, in the order the readers are
    // declared on the class: scheme, userInfo, host, port, path, query,
    // fragment. `path` is the one that is not nullable, so it is the one row
    // that needs no absent-before-present rule.
    public static function compare(string $left, string $right, int $index): int {
        var $a = Core\Uri::parse($left);
        var $b = Core\Uri::parse($right);
        if ($index == 0) { return Part::texts(Part::fold($a->scheme()), Part::fold($b->scheme())); }
        if ($index == 1) { return Part::texts($a->userInfo(), $b->userInfo()); }
        if ($index == 2) { return Part::texts(Part::fold($a->host()), Part::fold($b->host())); }
        if ($index == 3) { return Part::ports($a->port(), $b->port()); }
        if ($index == 4) { return Core\Str::compare($a->path(), $b->path()); }
        if ($index == 5) { return Part::texts($a->query(), $b->query()); }
        return Part::texts($a->fragment(), $b->fragment());
    }

    // The index of the first reader that disagrees, or `7` when none does.
    public static function decider(string $left, string $right): int {
        int $index = 0;
        while ($index < 7) {
            if (Part::compare($left, $right, $index) != 0) {
                return $index;
            }
            $index = $index + 1;
        }
        return 7;
    }

    // What the seven readers say the answer has to be.
    public static function predict(string $left, string $right): int {
        int $first = Part::decider($left, $right);
        if ($first == 7) {
            return 0;
        }
        return Part::compare($left, $right, $first);
    }
}

// Nine references: one base, one differing from it only in what § 6.2.2.1
// folds, and one per reader position. The port row is `:9` against `:8080`,
// where a text ordering answers the opposite of a numeric one.
array<string> $corpus = [
    "http://u@a.example:8080/p?q#f",
    "HTTP://u@A.EXAMPLE:8080/p?q#f",
    "https://u@a.example:8080/p?q#f",
    "http://v@a.example:8080/p?q#f",
    "http://u@b.example:8080/p?q#f",
    "http://u@a.example:9/p?q#f",
    "http://u@a.example:8080/z?q#f",
    "http://u@a.example:8080/p?r#f",
    "http://u@a.example:8080/p?q#g",
];

// All 81 ordered pairs. The prediction is built out of the readers alone and
// never looks at `compareTo`, so an agreement on every pair is the statement
// that the member is the composition of its parts.
int $pairs = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($corpus as string $left) {
    foreach ($corpus as string $right) {
        $pairs = $pairs + 1;
        if (Core\Uri::parse($left)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($right)) == Part::predict($left, $right)) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "pairs ", $pairs, " agreeing with the readers ", $agreed, "\n";

// The prediction is only worth as much as the corpus reaches, so the second
// half says which reader decided the base against each of the other eight:
// `7` for the base itself and for the pair § 6.2.2.1 makes equivalent, then
// `0` through `6` once each.
// Every position in the tuple gets to decide, which is what stops the count
// above from being satisfied by a member that only ever looks at the scheme.
foreach ($corpus as string $other) {
    echo Part::decider("http://u@a.example:8080/p?q#f", $other);
}
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
pairs 81 agreeing with the readers 81
770123456
